  "live_records": {
    "q-171954": {
      "dom_id": "step-q171954",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14195367/assets/f2c8ddcb1cddce59fbecf25ea606e8400d04d896dfdb263dd0aaf4e6c7de682f.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is{{field-1}}\n${cm}^{2}.$\n(Figure not drawn to scale)",
      "worked_solution": "The area of the triangle is defined as \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n where $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the base is \n\n$$\nb=AB=12\\,\\text{cm}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding height is \n\n$$\nh=6\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 6}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{72}{2} \\\\  & =36\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
