    "q-21055": {
      "dom_id": "step-q21055",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14195367/assets/b12eaaaf9aafa591bef00ca1b11d24783fae4a46f7253bddb05b9b206dbcb9e6.png)\n\nFind $HB$ given that the area of $△ABC$ is $140\\,{m}^{2},$  $AH=15\\,m,$ and $CH=14\\,m.$\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)",
      "worked_solution": "The area of the triangle is defined as \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n where $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the base is $b=AB,$ and the corresponding height is \n\n$$\nh=CH=14\\,m.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the formula for the area, we can solve for the base as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 140 & =\\frac{b\\cdot 14}{2} \\\\ 280 & =b\\cdot 14 \\\\ b & =\\frac{280}{14} \\\\ b & =20\\,m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $AB=20\\,m$ and hence, we have\n\n$$\nHB=AB-AH=20-15=5\\,m.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
